## TL;DR;

```bash
helm install --generate-name --wait oci://ghcr.io/sftpgo/helm-charts/sftpgo
```

## Configuration

SFTPGo has an extensive set of [configuration](https://docs.sftpgo.com/latest/config-file/) options allowing you to control the large set of features it provides.

The following options are available to configure SFTPGo when installing it with this chart.

**Note:** environmental configurations (like port bindings, certain directories, etc) are configured by the chart or the container image using flags and environment variables and they cannot be configured using a config file.

### values.yaml

Setting the `config` key in the values file is the easiest way to configure SFTPGo:

```yaml
config:
    sftpd:
        max_auth_tries: 10
```

### Custom volume mount

A custom configuration file can be mounted using the `volumes` and `volumeMounts` keys (see [Values](#values)).

By default, SFTPGo looks for a configuration file named `sftpgo` (with any supported extension, e.g. `sftpgo.json`, `sftpgo.yaml`) in the following locations, in order of precedence (the first file found wins):

- the configuration directory, which defaults to the current working directory, `/var/lib/sftpgo` (it can be changed using the `SFTPGO_CONFIG_DIR` environment variable)
- `$HOME/.config/sftpgo`, that is `/var/lib/sftpgo/.config/sftpgo` since `/var/lib/sftpgo` is the home directory of the `sftpgo` user
- `/etc/sftpgo` (the chart mounts the configuration generated from `values.yaml` to `/etc/sftpgo/sftpgo.json`)
- `/usr/local/etc/sftpgo`

You can mount a config map or a secret to `/var/lib/sftpgo/.config/sftpgo`.

**Note:** this method will override all configuration set in `values.yaml`.

**Example:**

```yaml
# configmap.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: custom-sftpgo-config
data:
  sftpgo.yaml: |-
    sftpd:
        max_auth_tries: 10
```

```yaml
# values.yaml

volumes:
  - name: custom-config # config is already taken
    configMap:
      name: custom-sftpgo-config

volumeMounts:
  - name: custom-config # config is already taken
    mountPath: /var/lib/sftpgo/.config/sftpgo
```

Alternatively, you can mount the config file to any arbitrary location (except `/etc/sftpgo`) and set the `SFTPGO_CONFIG_FILE` environment variable (using `env` or `envFrom`, see [Values](#values)).

### Custom services

The primary service created by the chart includes every enabled server (including HTTP and telemetry).
This can be a problem when you want to expose specific (but not all) servers to the internet using a `LoadBalancer` type service.

The `services` option in the values file allows you to create custom services enabling specific server ports.

The following example exposes the SFTP server (and **only** the SFTP server) using a `LoadBalancer` service:

```yaml
services:
  sftp-public:
    annotations:
      external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/hostname: sftp.mydomain.com.
    labels:
      environment: production
    type: LoadBalancer
    ports:
      sftp:
        port: 22
```

Additional services accept the same options as the `service` option in the values file and
require at least one port.

If your cluster runs more than one load balancer implementation, set `loadBalancerClass`
(on `service` or on any entry under `services`) to choose which one handles the service.
The field only applies to `LoadBalancer` services and is immutable: changing it requires recreating the service.

```yaml
services:
  sftp-public:
    type: LoadBalancer
    loadBalancerClass: metallb.io/metallb
    ports:
      sftp:
        port: 22
```

### Additional ports

Besides the built-in servers (`sftp`, `ftp`, `webdav`, `http`), the `ports` of `service` and of any entry
under `services` accept other keys. Each key is the name of a service port with these options:

- `port`: service port. Required, unless the key matches an `additionalPorts` entry (see below).
- `targetPort`: container port, by name or number. Defaults to the key, which must match a named
  container port: a built-in server, `telemetry`, an `additionalPorts` entry or a port of an extra
  container (or of a sidecar init container, with `restartPolicy: Always`).
- `nodePort`: node port, for `NodePort` and `LoadBalancer` services.
- `appProtocol`: defaults to the `additionalPorts` entry with the same key, if any, or to the
  application protocol of the targeted SFTPGo port.

All the ports use the TCP protocol. Keys are service port names: lowercase letters, digits and hyphens. A key set to `null` is ignored,
so a values file can remove a port defined by another one.

The following example exposes SFTP on both port 22 and port 2200:

```yaml
services:
  sftp-public:
    type: LoadBalancer
    ports:
      sftp:
        port: 22
      sftp-alt:
        port: 2200
        targetPort: sftp
```

When SFTPGo listens on more ports, for example with additional bindings, declare them under
`additionalPorts`. They are added to the container and to the main service, and additional services
can expose them by listing their name: `port` defaults to the `additionalPorts` entry, `nodePort` is not
inherited because node ports are allocated cluster-wide. The following example adds an FTP binding with implicit TLS
on container port 2990 and exposes it on port 990:

```yaml
ftpd:
  enabled: true

env:
  SFTPGO_FTPD__BINDINGS__1__PORT: "2990"
  SFTPGO_FTPD__BINDINGS__1__TLS_MODE: "2" # requires a TLS certificate, configured as usual

additionalPorts:
  ftps:
    containerPort: 2990
    port: 990

services:
  ftps-public:
    type: LoadBalancer
    ftpPassive: true
    ports:
      ftps: {} # port 990, targetPort ftps
```

### FTP passive ports

SFTPGo uses a single passive port range, shared by all the FTP bindings, set with `ftpd.passivePortRange`.
The chart sets it with environment variables, which take precedence over `passive_port_range` in `config`.
The server announces the port number to clients, so passive ports cannot be remapped: the chart exposes
each port of the range with the same service and container port, and for `NodePort` services also as
node port (the range must be within the node port range of the cluster). The default range (30050-30070)
is within the default node port range. Node ports are allocated cluster-wide: only one `NodePort` service,
across all the releases in the cluster, can expose the passive ports of a given range.

The main service exposes the passive ports when the FTP server is enabled (set `service.ftpPassive: false`
to disable this). Additional services expose them only when `ftpPassive: true` is set, even if they do not
expose the `ftp` port, as in the FTPS example above.

`service.ports.ftp.passiveRange` and `services.<name>.ports.ftp.passiveRange` are deprecated.
The former is still honored if `ftpd.passivePortRange` has its default value, otherwise the two must match.
The latter enables the passive ports of the additional service and must match the passive port range.
See the [changelog](https://github.com/sftpgo/helm-chart/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md) for upgrade notes.

Note that each passive port is a separate service port: some load balancer implementations limit
the number of ports per service.

### Gateway API

The chart can expose SFTPGo via [Kubernetes Gateway API](https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/) resources
in addition to (or instead of) Ingress. `HTTPRoute` is used for UI, REST API and WebDAV; `TCPRoute`
is used for SFTP and FTP.

**Prerequisites**

- A `Gateway` resource must already be configured in the cluster. The chart only creates `*Route`
  resources and attaches them to an existing Gateway via `parentRefs`.
- `HTTPRoute` is part of the Gateway API **standard** channel (GA in `gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1`,
  Gateway API v1.0, October 2023). Any conformant controller supports it.
- `TCPRoute` is part of the **standard** channel since Gateway API v1.6 (`gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1`);
  older releases only provide it in the **experimental** channel (`v1alpha2`). The chart uses `v1`
  when the cluster serves it and falls back to `v1alpha2` otherwise. With `helm template`, which
  does not query the cluster, pass `--api-versions gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1/TCPRoute` to render
  `v1`. The controller must support TCPRoute.
  Controller support varies: for example Istio and Envoy Gateway support it; some controllers
  (e.g. upstream Nginx Gateway Fabric) do not.

**Path-prefix semantics**

Understanding how SFTPGo serves its different surfaces helps pick the right `pathPrefix` and
filters:

| Surface | Served at | Notes |
|---------|-----------|-------|
| Web UI (admin + client) | `{httpd.web_root}/web/...` | Static asset URLs are rewritten by SFTPGo to include `web_root`, so the external path must match. Set `pathPrefix` equal to `httpd.web_root` (default `/`). |
| REST API | `/api/v2/...` | Always at `/api`, regardless of `web_root`. To expose the API at a different external prefix, set `pathPrefix` and add a `URLRewrite` filter (see below). |
| WebDAV | `{webdavd.bindings[].prefix}/...` | Similar to UI: set `pathPrefix` to match the configured binding prefix (default `/`). |

**Example: UI and API behind the same Gateway**

```yaml
httpd:
  enabled: true

gatewayApi:
  httpRoutes:
    ui:
      enabled: true
      hostnames:
        - sftpgo.example.com
      parentRefs:
        - name: my-gateway
          namespace: gateway-system
          sectionName: https
      # pathPrefix defaults to "/" — matches the default httpd.web_root
    api:
      enabled: true
      hostnames:
        - sftpgo.example.com
      parentRefs:
        - name: my-gateway
          namespace: gateway-system
          sectionName: https
      # pathPrefix defaults to "/api" — no rewrite needed
```

**Example: API exposed at a custom external prefix via URLRewrite**

SFTPGo does not apply `web_root` to the REST API, so to expose it at `/sftpgo/api` externally you
need to strip the `/sftpgo` prefix before the request reaches the backend:

```yaml
gatewayApi:
  httpRoutes:
    api:
      enabled: true
      hostnames:
        - gw.example.com
      parentRefs:
        - name: my-gateway
          namespace: gateway-system
      pathPrefix: /sftpgo/api
      filters:
        - type: URLRewrite
          urlRewrite:
            path:
              type: ReplacePrefixMatch
              replacePrefixMatch: /api
```

**Example: SFTP via TCPRoute**

```yaml
sftpd:
  enabled: true

gatewayApi:
  tcpRoutes:
    sftp:
      enabled: true
      parentRefs:
        - name: my-gateway
          namespace: gateway-system
          sectionName: sftp
```

Note: for FTP, only the control port is routed. Passive data ports (`ftpd.passivePortRange`)
require separate handling (typically a dedicated `LoadBalancer` Service) because Gateway API does
not model port ranges.

